- Perimeter loop in C++: `double dx = path[j].x - path[i].x;` — the subtraction happens in int64 then converts to double; replicate exactly (i64 subtraction then `as f64`).
- `totalVertices += path.size()` — i64; score = totalArea*0.7 + totalPerimeter*0.2 + totalVertices*0.1 (totalVertices as f64 via C++ implicit conversion).
- `complexityScore` printed via std::cout default formatting (hence cppfmt).
- Match C++ evaluation order where it affects floating point (sums in the same order).

You can't fully compile /output/test20.rs until the other two modules land. To validate YOUR code meanwhile: create a scratch dir /tmp/drv with stub modules clipper.rs (trivial impls of the API above) and offset.rs (inflate_paths returning input), symlink or `#[path]` them, compile `rustc --edition 2021` a copy of your test20.rs against the stubs to prove it compiles, and separately unit-test cppfmt thoroughly against real printf: compile a tiny C program or use the reference values — e.g. verify format_cpp_double(14892.7…)→"14892.7", 34088.0→"34088", 0.0→"0", and generate random doubles and compare against `printf "%g"` via a small C helper compiled with cc (check `which cc gcc`). Aim for byte-identical %g output on millions of random doubles if feasible.

